## DESCRIPTION
##   Using R to find descriptive statistics
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Exploratory data analysis/descriptive statistics)
## DBsection(Summary statistics)
## Date(01/01/15)
## Institution(The College of Idaho)
## Author(R Cruz)
## Level(2)
## KEYWORDS('Descriptive Statistics')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',  'PGML.pl',
    'parserPopUp.pl', 'contextUnits.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context('Units')->addUnits('texts' => { text => 1, aliases => ['text'] });

#This makes the connected problems have the same seed
SRAND($psvn);

@data = num_sort(map { random(5, 50) } (1 .. 5));
$sum  = 0;
$sum += $_ for (@data);

$median = $data[2];
$mean   = $sum / 5;

$outlier        = random(120, 300);
$median_outlier = ($data[2] + $data[3]) / 2;
$mean_outlier   = ($sum + $outlier) / 6;

@x = @data[ random_subset(5, 0 .. 4) ];

$popup = DropDown(
    [
        "Neither are affected", "Both are equally affected",
        "Median",               "Mean"
    ],
    "Mean"
);

BEGIN_PGML
The table below gives the number of text messages five students receive on a particular day:
  
[@ DataTable(
  [
    [['Number of texts', headerrow => 1,noencase=>1],@x],
  ], 
  encase => ['\(','\)'],
  align => '| c | c | c| c | c |c|',
  midrules => 1
); @]*
  
In the previous problem you calculated the mean and median for the data set given above. In addition, you calculated the mean and median for the data set with an additional data point of [`[$outlier]`] included.


   
Which value was *more affected* by the outlier of [`[$outlier]`], the mean or the median?

[_]{$popup}
*Note: Be careful -- you only have one attempt for this one!*

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
With the original data: [`\bar{x} = [$mean]`] and the median was [`[$median]`].

With the outlier: [`\bar{x} = [$mean_outlier]`] and the median was [`[$median_outlier]`].

Then [$popup] was most affected by the outlier.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
